<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">James Monroe</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1758">1758</a>–<a href="/years/1831">1831</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">James Monroe was the fifth President of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, serving from 1817 to 1825, after earlier posts as governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a>, minister to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and secretary of state. His presidency saw the acquisition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Florida</a> and the Monroe Doctrine of 1823, which warned European powers against new colonisation in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>. He supported the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-colonization-society">American Colonization Society</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monrovia">Monrovia</a>, the capital of Liberia, is named after him.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/james-monroe.jpg" alt="James Monroe" width="320" height="398" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Samuel Finley Breese Morse · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:James_Monroe_White_House_portrait_1819_(cropped)(2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>James Monroe was born on 28 April 1758 in Westmoreland County in the Colony of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a>, the son of Andrew Spence Monroe, a craftsman who took part in protests against the Stamp Act, and Elizabeth Jones. In early 1776 he left college and joined the 3rd Virginia Regiment of the Continental Army as a lieutenant. At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-trenton">Battle of Trenton</a> he suffered a severed artery and nearly died; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-washington">George Washington</a> cited him for bravery and promoted him to captain. From 1780 to 1783 he studied law under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-jefferson">Thomas Jefferson</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Monroe sat in the Congress of the Confederation from November 1783, where he helped to write the Northwest Ordinance. He opposed the ratification of the Constitution, and in 1790 was elected to the Senate. Washington appointed him minister to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in 1794 but recalled him in 1796. He was governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a> from 1799 to 1802, and as Jefferson's envoy he helped to negotiate the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louisiana-purchase">Louisiana Purchase</a>. He fell out with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-madison">James Madison</a> over the Monroe–Pinkney Treaty, but in April 1811 became Madison's Secretary of State, and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-1812">War of 1812</a> also served as Secretary of War.</p><p>He defeated the Federalist Rufus King in 1816, and his inauguration on 4 March 1817 opened what was called the Era of Good Feelings, as the Federalists collapsed; in 1820 he was re-elected unopposed. He signed the Missouri Compromise, and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adams-onis-treaty">Adams–Onís Treaty</a> of 1819 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> secured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Florida</a> and a border across the continent.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After his term ended in 1825 Monroe lived at Oak Hill in Aldie, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a>, and was beset by financial difficulties. His wife Elizabeth died in 1830, and he moved to New York City, where he died on 4 July 1831. Monroe owned dozens of enslaved people, and sold property, including enslaved people, to pay debts; polls of historians tend to rank him as an above-average president. He was a member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-colonization-society">American Colonization Society</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monrovia">Monrovia</a>, the capital of Liberia, is named after him. He was the last president to wear the powdered wig and knee-breeches of the late 18th century.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “James Monroe” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After a failed first attempt on Sherbro Island in 1820, the society’s agents acquired land at Cape Mesurado in December 1821, where from 1822 the settlement later named Monrovia, after President <b>James Monroe</b>, was established.</blockquote><a href="/empire/liberia">Liberia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The settlement was named Monrovia after President <b>James Monroe</b>, and the colony was named Liberia in 1824.</blockquote><a href="/state/colony-of-liberia">Colony of Liberia</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Monroe Hall, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. Died in New York City, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=38.24,-76.99&amp;year=1805" aria-label="Monroe Hall on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2634 790 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of James Monroe"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3084 1060h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3084 1060h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=38.24,-76.99&amp;year=1805">What was here in 1805? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…the American continents, by the free and independent condition which they have assumed and maintain, are henceforth not to be considered as subjects for future colonization by any European powers.&#8221;</p><footer>Annual message to Congress (the Monroe Doctrine), 1823 &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://avalon.law.yale.edu/19th_century/monroe.asp" rel="noopener">The Avalon Project</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…we should consider any attempt on their part to extend their system to any portion of this hemisphere as dangerous to our peace and safety.&#8221;</p><footer>Annual message to Congress (the Monroe Doctrine), 1823 &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://avalon.law.yale.edu/19th_century/monroe.asp" rel="noopener">The Avalon Project</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;National honor is national property of the highest value. The sentiment in the mind of every citizen is national strength. It ought therefore to be cherished.&#8221;</p><footer>First Inaugural Address, 1817 &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/925/pg925-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
